"""
Multi-Sheet Excel Merger Studio  (v3.0)

Merges .xlsx / .xlsm / .xlsb / .xls / .csv files from one folder into a single
workbook, sheet by sheet: data with the same sheet name is stacked on top of
each other, regardless of whether it comes from an Excel file or a CSV file.

TWO MERGE ENGINES
-----------------
1. Standard (pandas + openpyxl)
   Fast, data-only merge. Finds the header row, removes empty rows, can skip
   rows at the top / bottom of every sheet and can add a source-file column.
   Formulas and formatting are NOT preserved (values only).

2. "Merge like human" (Microsoft Excel through COM, Windows only)
   Does what a person does with Ctrl+A -> Ctrl+C -> Ctrl+V: whole sheets are
   copied cell for cell into the output workbook. NOTHING is removed - all
   headers, formulas, number formats, colours, borders, merged cells,
   conditional formatting and column widths are kept. Requires desktop Excel
   and the `pywin32` package. A private, invisible Excel instance is used, so
   an Excel window you already have open is never touched.

CSV FILES
---------
A CSV file has no sheet name, so the program decides where it belongs
(in this order):
  1. FILE NAME  - the file name contains the name of a sheet found in the Excel
                  files ("Report_Sales.csv" -> sheet "Sales"; longest name wins).
  2. HEADERS    - the CSV columns match the columns of an Excel sheet (>= 80 %).
  3. NEW SHEET  - otherwise CSV files with identical columns are grouped into a
                  new sheet named after the common part of their file names.
Every decision is written to the TXT report.
(In "Merge like human" mode CSV files are appended after the Excel files.)

After every merge a TXT report is written next to the output file: rows read,
rows removed (with a breakdown) and rows in the output, plus a balance check.

Required : pandas, openpyxl
Optional : xlrd (.xls), pyxlsb (.xlsb), pywin32 ("Merge like human" mode)
"""
